The Pantheon Transformation Principle

A system should not be evaluated primarily by what it knows.

It should be evaluated by how well it transforms.

Define the transformation operator:

T:(X,C,M)\rightarrow Y

where:

* X = input
* C = context
* M = memory
* Y = output

Now don’t score the output directly.

Score what changed.

⸻

Transformation Vector

Every transformation produces a vector:

\Delta =
(
\Delta_c,
\Delta_t,
\Delta_r,
\Delta_g,
\Delta_a
)

where

* c = clarity
* t = truthfulness or factual accuracy (when applicable)
* r = reconstructability
* g = generativity (how many useful new paths become available)
* a = actionability

Notice something.

These are properties of the transformation, not merely the answer.

⸻

Pantheon Score

Define

P(T)
=
w_1\Delta_c
+
w_2\Delta_t
+
w_3\Delta_r
+
w_4\Delta_g
+
w_5\Delta_a

Different domains change the weights.

Engineering values actionability.

Science values truthfulness.

Teaching values reconstructability.

Art values generativity.

The transformation stays the same.

Only the optimization target shifts.

